/-!
# Cohen–Macaulay sheaves under a finite pushforward

Let `f : X ⟶ Y` be a finite morphism of schemes locally of finite type over a field `k`, and let
`F` be a coherent sheaf on `X` whose support `Supp F = {x | F_x ≠ 0}` is pure `r`-dimensional:
every irreducible component of `Supp F` has dimension `r` (the condition is vacuous when the
support is empty). Then for every point `y ∈ Y` the stalk `(f_* F)_y` is a Cohen–Macaulay
`𝒪_{Y,y}`-module if and only if `F_x` is a Cohen–Macaulay `𝒪_{X,x}`-module for every point `x`
of the fibre `f⁻¹(y)`; consequently `F` is a Cohen–Macaulay sheaf if and only if `f_* F` is.

The argument is the one of Kollár–Mori. Choose an affine open `V ∋ y`; then `U = f⁻¹V` is
affine, `R = Γ(Y, V) → S = Γ(X, U)` is finite, `A = 𝒪_{Y,y} = R_𝔭` and `M = (f_* F)_y = N_𝔭`
for the finite `S`-module `N = Γ(F, U)`. The semilocal ring `B = S_𝔭` (the localization of `S`
at the image of `R ∖ 𝔭`) has as maximal ideals the primes of `S` over `𝔭`, that is the points
of the fibre, with `B_𝔓 = 𝒪_{X,x}` and `M_𝔓 = F_x`. The depth of `M` over `A` is the minimum of
the depths of the nonzero `F_x` (`MMP.Mori.localDepth_eq_iInf_localDepth_localization`), and
`dim M` is the maximum of the `dim F_x`, so `M` is Cohen–Macaulay if and only if every `F_x` is,
as soon as all the nonzero `F_x` have the same dimension.

That last condition is where purity enters. `dim F_x` is the coheight of `x` in `Supp F`, and on
a scheme locally of finite type over a field the coheight of `x` in a closed set all of whose
components have dimension `r` is `r - dim closure{x}`
(`MMP.height_eq_height_add_coheight_Iic`). A finite morphism
preserves `dim closure{x}`: it is closed and its fibres are discrete, so it lifts and reflects
chains of specializations.

## Main results

* `MMP.Mori.isCMModule_stalk_pushforward_iff`: the pointwise form of the Cohen–Macaulay part of
  Kollár–Mori, Proposition 5.4.
* `MMP.Mori.isCMSheaf_pushforward_iff`: the Cohen–Macaulay part of Kollár–Mori,
  Proposition 5.4.

## Implementation notes

Cohen–Macaulay is `MMP.Mori.IsCMModule`, under which the zero module is Cohen–Macaulay, and
`MMP.Mori.IsCMSheaf` asks it of every stalk. The support of `F` is the set of points at which the
stalk is nontrivial, and the dimension of an irreducible component is its topological Krull
dimension, which is the dimension of the component with its reduced structure. The schemes are
assumed locally of finite type over `k`; `f` is not required to be a `k`-morphism, since a finite
morphism preserves the dimensions of closures of points regardless.

## References

* [J. Kollár, S. Mori, *Birational geometry of algebraic varieties*][KollarMori], 5.4
-/

